=== FAIL: zero-value rendering contradicts the codebase's own documented
contract for these exact two fields ===

Two pre-existing, already-committed files (not part of T14's diff, so not
something the worker could claim was "someone else's problem to fix")
explicitly document the required UI treatment of a zero value in these
fields:

  internal/services/retirement/analysis/tax_optimizer.go:224-226
    "Both are reporting-only columns; neither participates in ranking. They
    stay zero for a projection that never converts or never records a
    rate, and the UI renders "—" for zero."

  internal/services/retirement/analysis/tax_optimizer_reporting_test.go:77-78
    "A candidate that never converts must report zero, not a stale or
    planned figure — the UI renders "—" for zero."

This is stated twice, unambiguously, as the intended contract for exactly
the two fields T14 was dispatched to render. The actual template does the
opposite:

  web/templates/components/whatif/tax-optimizer.html
    <td ...>{{printf "%.0f" $c.PeakMarginalBracket}}%</td>
    <td ...>{{formatMoney $c.TotalRothConverted}}</td>

`formatMoney` has no zero special-case (internal/templates/render.go:422-449
— `formatMoney(0)` returns literal `"$0.00"`), and `printf "%.0f"` on 0
renders `"0%"`. Neither the headline nor the table body nor the glossary
has any dash/em-dash handling.

Worse, this isn't an oversight the worker was unaware of — T14's own new
test explicitly asserts the reversed behavior and calls out that it is
deliberate:

  internal/handlers/whatif/tax_optimizer_render_test.go:93-96
    "// Zero-value candidate must render $0.00, not blank.
    if !strings.Contains(out, "$0.00") {
        t.Errorf("expected zero TotalRothConverted to render as $0.00; ..."

So the worker built a fixture specifically to cover the zero case (a
"No conversions" candidate, TotalRothConverted: 0), noticed the zero case
mattered, and encoded the opposite of the documented contract as a passing
assertion — with no comment reconciling the two, no note that the earlier
contract was being revised, and nothing in the T14 task description
(as given to me) that authorizes overriding it.

This is not a corner case: a "no conversion" candidate is a normal, likely
common row in the Top-5 table (the fixture itself models it as the
baseline), so real users will regularly see "$0.00" / "0%" in cells that
the codebase's own design intent says should read "—". A user glancing at
"$0.00" next to "Roth converted" next to a strategy that in fact skips
conversion may reasonably read it as "the engine computed $0" rather than
"not applicable," which is exactly the ambiguity the original comment was
written to avoid ("not a stale or planned figure").

Given `.swarm/critical.globs`-style reasoning aside, this is a genuine,
concrete contradiction between what the codebase already says the feature
must do and what was shipped, discovered by comparing files outside the
two the manifest claims to touch — the kind of cross-file contract check a
same-file diff review would miss. Per FAIL-on-ambiguity: this is not
ambiguous, it's a direct textual contradiction, so FAIL stands.

Recommendation: either implement the "—" rendering for zero (guard both
the `%.0f` and `formatMoney` calls, headline included) and update the test
to assert the dash, or — if the dollar/percent zero display is now the
intended design — update the two contract comments in tax_optimizer.go and
tax_optimizer_reporting_test.go to match and record that decision, so a
future reader isn't misled by stale documentation. Either resolution is
outside what T14 as claimed currently does.
